# Assets

## Your job is the manifest, not the API

You fill `assets/manifest.json`. You never call an image API directly, never
write a loop that generates images, never regenerate an asset because you think
it could be better. The script owns generation: `npm run assets` (add `--dry`
first to see the plan and the count).

Why: an LLM in a correction loop will regenerate twelve images per iteration
and the bill arrives at the end of the month. `MAX_IMAGES_PER_RUN` exists to
make that impossible, and you do not raise it on your own.

## What may be generated

Decorative heroes, textures, abstract illustration, section backgrounds,
pictograms, patterns.

## What may never be generated

- Faces presented as real people: testimonial portraits, team photos, "our
  technician" shots. This is a false-advertising exposure for the client and
  visitors spot it immediately. If the brief needs these, the answer is "the
  client must supply photos", and you say so out loud.
- Anything depicting the client's actual premises, products, vehicles or
  certifications.
- Logos, brand marks, or anything resembling an existing company's identity.

## Prompt quality

A usable prompt names: subject, medium, lighting, composition, and an explicit
exclusion list. `defaults.style` carries the shared art direction so the whole
set looks like one family. If two assets do not look related, fix the shared
style block, not the individual prompts.

## After generation

Reference `/assets/<id>.webp`. Every image gets real alt text describing what
it shows, or `alt=""` plus `aria-hidden` if it is purely decorative. Filler alt
(`"image"`, `"hero"`, `"photo"`) fails the M-13 gate and blocks the round.
